atom     signatures/definedness-not-enforced
source   curated
kind     binding — a `:D` constraint must reject a type object

# The `:D` smiley constrains a parameter to a DEFINED value: an instance, not a
# type object. Rakudo rejects the binding; rakupp binds the type object and runs
# the body. Documented behaviour, not a matter of taste — so the assertion is
# Rakudo's answer, and rakupp's is recorded as the divergence it is.
#
# Arguments go through a capture so the question stays a RUNTIME binding one.
# Passed literally, Rakudo settles some of these at compile time instead, and a
# compile error is a different observation from a binding failure.
#
# `rejected` / `bound` rather than an exception type, deliberately: the two
# engines disagree about the exception's CLASS as well (see the sibling atom),
# and this atom is about whether the constraint is enforced at all.

- id     0001
  from   review:2026-08-18 · found sampling gen/signatures.raku before its first run
  code   my $A = \(Int); sub f(Int:D $a) { "bound" }; (try f(|$A)) // "rejected"
  is     "rejected"
  oracle rakudo-2026.07 → "rejected"
  oracle rakupp-v3.14.0-107-gb8ea9bb-modified → "bound"

- id     0002
  from   review:2026-08-18
  code   my $A = \(Any); sub f(Any:D $a) { "bound" }; (try f(|$A)) // "rejected"
  is     "rejected"
  oracle rakudo-2026.07 → "rejected"
  oracle rakupp-v3.14.0-107-gb8ea9bb-modified → "bound"

- id     0003
  from   review:2026-08-18 · the control — a DEFINED argument must bind on both
  code   my $A = \(1); sub f(Int:D $a) { "bound" }; (try f(|$A)) // "rejected"
  is     "bound"
  oracle rakudo-2026.07 → "bound"
  oracle rakupp-v3.14.0-107-gb8ea9bb-modified → "bound"

- id     0004
  from   review:2026-08-18 · a method binds by the same rules as a sub
  code   my $A = \(Int); my $C = class { method m(Int:D $a) { "bound" } }; (try $C.m(|$A)) // "rejected"
  is     "rejected"
  oracle rakudo-2026.07 → "rejected"
  oracle rakupp-v3.14.0-107-gb8ea9bb-modified → "bound"

- id     0005
  from   review:2026-08-18 · a default does not excuse an argument that was passed
  code   my $A = \(Int); sub f(Int:D $a = 7) { "bound" }; (try f(|$A)) // "rejected"
  is     "rejected"
  oracle rakudo-2026.07 → "rejected"
  oracle rakupp-v3.14.0-107-gb8ea9bb-modified → "bound"
